## Abstract
Kerberos version 5 (RFC 4120) remains the foundational authentication backbone for enterprise infrastructures and directory services worldwide. However, standard Kerberos architectures suffer from a critical structural asymmetry: ticket issuance decisions are static and binary. Once authenticated, users are granted unconstrained service tickets—typically valid for 8 to 10 hours—which cannot be revoked prior to expiration. Consequently, adversaries who obtain valid credentials through phishing, password spraying, or offline Kerberoasting enjoy broad lateral movement across the network without intermediate challenge. While intrusion detection systems (IDS) analyze KDC audit logs, existing solutions operate strictly in an *alert-only* posture, creating an unmitigated exposure window between detection and human analyst remediation. Conversely, deploying complex anomaly-detection models directly on the synchronous authentication request path introduces prohibitive latency penalties (measured at 12.9 ms per request for tree-based models against a sub-millisecond KDC budget).

In this paper, we present **AZ-Kerberos**, a closed-loop, risk-adaptive ticket issuance system designed for stock MIT Kerberos without protocol modifications or custom client software. AZ-Kerberos decouples heavy anomaly scoring from synchronous ticket issuance through a three-tier architecture: (1) an asynchronous, log-driven scoring engine that evaluates sliding-window behavioral features via unsupervised Isolation Forests; (2) a lockless, memory-mapped binary risk table utilizing FNV-1a hashing with open-addressing linear probing; and (3) a lightweight C `kdcpolicy` plugin (RFC-compliant MIT krb5 pluggable interface) executing on the KDC request path that enforces adaptive ticket lifetime and renewal caps or request denials in **14.2 nanoseconds**. Furthermore, we formally identify and address the *Victim Lock-Out Dilemma* (Threat T1)—wherein attackers intentionally trigger failed authentications to deny legitimate users—through a multi-signal escalation rule and an automated realm circuit breaker. Finally, AZ-Kerberos binds every policy decision and table publication into an Ed25519-signed, hash-chained audit log achieving **64,476 events/second** throughput. Rigorous empirical evaluation on a live MIT Kerberos deployment demonstrates that AZ-Kerberos cuts attacker ticket acquisition during Kerberoasting by 89% while preserving fail-open operational resilience.

