#### Key Findings
1. **Mitigation of Kerberoasting:** Under an unprotected KDC, an adversary acquires all 18 service ticket hashes with full 10-hour validity, allowing uninterrupted offline cracking. Under AZ-Kerberos, the adversary obtains only 2 full tickets before the model detects anomalous service fan-out; subsequent requests are immediately capped to 10 minutes and then completely rejected (8 denied), starving the adversary of ticket ciphertexts.
2. **Superiority over Threshold Rules:** While a static threshold rule eventually caps tickets after a fixed count ($>10$ requests), it fails to reach denial, permitting the adversary to harvest more than three times as many valid tickets.
3. **Protection against Victim Lock-Out (T1):** Despite 15 legitimate accounts being aggressively targeted by external password failures, **zero legitimate principals were locked out**, validating our multi-signal escalation rule.

